{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "פרח", :translit "parach", :gloss "bloomed"} {:he "ציץ", :translit "tzitz", :gloss "blossom"} {:he "שקדים", :translit "shekadim", :gloss "almonds"} {:he "ג' שערערו על הכהונה", :translit "sheloshah she'ar'eru al ha-khunah", :gloss "three who disputed the priesthood"} {:he "קרח", :translit "korach", :gloss "Korah"} {:he "ירבעם", :translit "yarav'am", :gloss "Jeroboam"} {:he "עוזיהו", :translit "uzziyahu", :gloss "Uzziah"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "The word שקדים (almonds) corresponds to three figures who illegitimately challenged priestly authority."}], :frame {:text "Korah led a rebellion against the priesthood of Aaron (Numbers 16:3). Jeroboam appointed non-priests to serve at his rival shrines (I Kings 12:31, I Kings 13:33). Uzziah unlawfully entered the Temple to offer incense, for which he was struck with tzara'at (II Chronicles 26:16–20). These three episodes are cited in rabbinic tradition as the classic challenges to legitimate priestly prerogative; see e.g. Bavli Sanhedrin 101b and Berakhot 55a for the episode of Jeroboam's altar, and Sifrei Zuta 17:23 which pairs Korah's challenge with Uzziah's.", :sources ["Numbers 16:3" "I Kings 12:31" "I Kings 13:33" "II Chronicles 26:16–20" "Bavli Sanhedrin 101b" "Sifrei Zuta 17:23"]}} {:quotes [{:he "שקדים", :translit "shekadim", :gloss "almonds"} {:he "חשמונאים", :translit "chashmona'im", :gloss "Hasmoneans"} {:he "דמ\"ז", :translit "dalet-mem-zayin", :gloss "44+40+7"} {:he "ל\"ח", :translit "lamed-chet", :gloss "30+8"} {:he "שהם מזרע אהרן", :translit "she-hem mi-zera aharon", :gloss "that they are of the seed of Aaron"} {:he "ותתקיים הכהונה בידם", :translit "ve-titkayem ha-khunah be-yadam", :gloss "and the priesthood was fulfilled through their hands"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "שקדים", :b "חשמונים"}], :frame {:text "The Hasmonean family traced its lineage to Jehoiarib, a priestly division (I Chronicles 24:7). The books of Maccabees and rabbinic sources such as Bavli Shabbat 21b and the scholium to Megillat Ta'anit recount their rededication of the Temple. The Hasmonean period is noted for priestly leadership that combined political and religious authority (cf. Bavli Kiddushin 66a on Hasmonean kings).", :sources ["I Chronicles 24:7" "Bavli Shabbat 21b" "Bavli Kiddushin 66a"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "The word שקדים (almonds) corresponds to three figures who illegitimately challenged priestly authority.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :gematria, :a "שקדים", :a-value 454, :b "חשמונים", :b-value 454, :lens :standard, :by :selah, :equal? true}], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:29:44.359096081Z", :model "glm-5.2"}